RUNNING PYCHARMM UNDER MPI (mpi4py)
===================================

Default model: one independent serial CHARMM per rank
-----------------------------------------------------

pycharmm is normally driven with mpi4py in a single-program,
multiple-data (SPMD) style: every MPI rank runs the same Python script,
and each rank hosts its own complete, independent, SERIAL CHARMM.  Each
rank reads the whole system and computes its own energies and forces
locally; there is no domain decomposition across ranks.  Coordination
between ranks (exchanging coordinates, gathering energies, etc.) is done
explicitly by the script through mpi4py.  This is the natural fit for
ensemble methods -- replica exchange, free-energy windows (including
BLaDE, which is single-rank by design), string-method replicas, and so
on.

Concretely, inside pycharmm every rank sees:

    ?numnode = 1      and      ?mynode = 0

regardless of how many processes mpirun launched.  CHARMM is serial on
each rank, so its internal broadcasts and reductions do nothing, and one
rank can never block waiting on another inside CHARMM.

Practical rules
---------------

1. Get the rank and world size from mpi4py, NOT from CHARMM.  CHARMM's
   ?numnode / ?mynode are always 1 / 0 under pycharmm and do not
   describe the MPI world:

       from mpi4py import MPI
       comm = MPI.COMM_WORLD
       rank = comm.Get_rank()      # 0 .. N-1
       size = comm.Get_size()      # N

2. Name per-rank output and scratch files by the mpi4py rank, so ranks
   do not overwrite each other (e.g. f"out_{rank}.log", not a fixed
   name driven by ?mynode).

3. Import order does not matter.  Earlier versions could hang (the
   master rank printed while the others sat idle) if pycharmm was
   imported before mpi4py; that is fixed -- each rank now always comes
   up as a single-rank CHARMM on MPI_COMM_SELF.

A minimal SPMD example, run as "mpirun -np N python script.py":

    from mpi4py import MPI
    comm = MPI.COMM_WORLD
    rank = comm.Get_rank()

    from pycharmm import *
    # ... read rtf/prm, build or read the system (each rank does this) ...

    e = get_energy_value('ENER')          # this rank's own energy
    all_e = comm.gather(e, root=0)        # collect across ranks via mpi4py
    if rank == 0:
        print('energies from all ranks:', all_e)


Re-enabling a genuine multi-node CHARMM (advanced)
--------------------------------------------------

If instead you want CHARMM itself to run as one N-node parallel job
(MPI_COMM_WORLD, ?numnode = N) -- for example to decompose a single
system across ranks with CHARMM's own parallel force/energy machinery --
set the environment variable CHARMM_MULTINODE to a truthy value
(1, y, or t) before launching:

    CHARMM_MULTINODE=1 mpirun -np N -x CHARMM_MULTINODE python script.py

(The "-x CHARMM_MULTINODE" propagates the variable to every rank; some
launchers export the environment automatically.)

This is expert-level use and comes with a requirement: every rank must
issue IDENTICAL CHARMM commands in lockstep.  CHARMM's parallel model is
master/worker with collective communication, so any rank-dependent
CHARMM call (for example "if rank == 0: minimize.run_sd(...)") will
deadlock -- the master proceeds while the others wait for a broadcast
that never matches.  Use mpi4py for anything that must differ between
ranks; keep the CHARMM command stream the same everywhere.  When
CHARMM_MULTINODE is unset (the default), each rank is an independent
serial CHARMM as described above.


Groups of parallel CHARMMs: N x M (experimental)
------------------------------------------------

The two models above are the endpoints of a spectrum: N independent
serial CHARMMs (the default) and one N-node parallel CHARMM
(CHARMM_MULTINODE).  In between is an N x M layout -- N groups of M
ranks each, where every group is its own M-node parallel CHARMM.  This
is the natural shape for, e.g., replica exchange where each replica is
itself a multi-node parallel simulation.

You build it by handing CHARMM the communicator it should run on.  Use
mpi4py to split MPI_COMM_WORLD into N sub-communicators of M ranks, then
pass this rank's sub-communicator to CHARMM with set_mpi_comm() BEFORE
any other pycharmm call (the communicator is adopted when the CHARMM
library first initializes, so it must be set first):

    from mpi4py import MPI
    world = MPI.COMM_WORLD
    M = 2                                   # ranks per CHARMM group
    color = world.Get_rank() // M           # 0 .. N-1  (which group)
    group = world.Split(color, world.Get_rank() % M)

    import pycharmm
    pycharmm.set_mpi_comm(group)            # MUST precede any CHARMM call
    from pycharmm import *                   # now the library comes up on `group`

Inside each group CHARMM sees ?numnode = M and ?mynode = the group-local
rank (0 .. M-1), and its internal force/energy decomposition runs across
the M ranks of that group.  A value of M = 1 reproduces the default
per-rank-serial model (each group is a one-rank CHARMM on its own
communicator).

set_mpi_comm() has the same lockstep requirement as CHARMM_MULTINODE:
within a group every rank must issue IDENTICAL CHARMM commands, and you
use mpi4py (not CHARMM) for anything that must differ between groups.

EXPERIMENTAL -- known limitation.  For M > 1 the group runs CHARMM's
master/worker command model: the group master reads and broadcasts each
command while the workers receive it.  pycharmm drives CHARMM one
command at a time from Python on every rank, and reconciling that
per-command Python execution with the master/worker broadcast stream is
not yet complete -- multi-command scripts on M > 1 groups can desync
(typically hanging at shutdown).  Until that work lands (a dedicated
worker-loop rework), treat M > 1 as experimental.  M = 1 (the N x 1
default, with or without set_mpi_comm) is fully supported.

A note on MPI ownership.  Whoever initializes MPI finalizes it.  When
mpi4py is imported first it owns MPI, and CHARMM leaves MPI_Finalize to
mpi4py's own atexit teardown; CHARMM only calls MPI_Finalize when it was
CHARMM that called MPI_Init.  You therefore do not need to (and should
not) call MPI.Finalize() by hand -- let mpi4py's normal shutdown run.


